{{see|Haben}}
==German==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|habēn}} < {{PG.|de}} *{{term||khaf-}} < {{PIE.|de}} *{{term||kap-|to grasp}}. Akin to Old Saxon {{term|hebbjan}}, Old Norse {{term|hafa}} (Swedish {{term|hava}}/{{term|ha|lang=sv}}), Old Frisian {{term|habba}}, Gothic {{term|haban}}.  Cognate with English {{term|have}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhaːbən/|lang=de}}
*{{audio|De-haben.ogg|audio}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|hat|hatte|gehabt}}
<!-- Some sources call "haben" a strong verb since it has a stem change.  Others just call it "irregular" since its change does not involve the vowel. -->

# {{transitive}} To [[have]]; to [[possess]]; to [[own]].
# {{transitive}} To [[have]]; to [[hold]]; to [[contain]].
# {{transitive|auxiliary|with a past participle|lang=de}} To [[have]]; {{non-gloss definition|forms the [[perfect tense|perfect]] and [[past perfect|past perfect tense]]}}

====Derived terms====

* [[Habe]]
* [[Habsucht]]
* [[Haben]]
* [[Habgier]]
* [[Habseligkeit]]
* [[habhaft]]

